    fix(transport): stop a log call taking down its caller

    emit() ended in a bare JSON.stringify, which throws on a circular
    reference and on a BigInt. One call site passes a value the consumer
    controls: connectionHandler logs userId, which is whatever the configured
    WsAuthenticator returned. The type says string | number, but an
    authenticator is consumer-supplied and unchecked at runtime, and a
    realistic one returns a database row or an ORM document - both of which
    routinely carry a back-reference.

    The throw landed between sessionStore.create and the registration of the
    close listener, so it did more than lose a line. Reproduced end to end
    against dist/, with an authenticator returning an identity whose id
    closes a cycle:

      before: client saw open, then "Invalid WebSocket frame: RSV1 must be
              clear", then close 1006; sessions left in the store: 1
      after:  client saw open, then the connected frame, then a clean close;
              sessions left in the store: 0

    The 500 reaches the client as a malformed frame because rejectSocket
    writes a raw HTTP status line onto a socket that has already been
    upgraded. The session is orphaned because ws.on('close') is registered
    thirty lines below the throw. With the default in-memory store that is
    one leaked session per connection attempt, unbounded, with every
    connection failing.

    serialise() now falls back to a line carrying the four envelope keys and
    a data string naming the reason, so an operator can tell a cycle from a
    BigInt rather than reading "[unserialisable]" and guessing. The probe is
    split out of the fallback to keep re-lint/no-catch-as-control-flow
    satisfied without a suppression: it returns a status rather than using
    the catch as a branch.

    This is the hazard the onConnect comment three lines below already names
    - "a synchronous throw in the connection listener corrupts an
    already-upgraded socket". That was guarded for the consumer hook while
    the log call above it was not.

